This looks like mid-2014. Unemployment is 6.3%, which fits April-June 2014 (April 2014 was 6.3%). The fed funds rate is about 0.09%, and the 10-year yield is 2.45%, which fits May 2014, when the 10-year was around 2.45%. Headline CPI at 1.96% also fits April or May 2014 (CPI was 2.0% in April and 2.1% in May). Gold's 12-month change of -11.9% fits May 2014, since gold was about $1,250 against about $1,400 a year earlier. Equities were up 2.3% in the month, which matches May 2014 (S&P +2.4%). The 3-month average payroll gain of 237k fits April 2014, when the 3-month average was about 240k, so the data might be as of May with the April jobs report. The unemployment rate was 6.3% in both April and May, and the 3-month change of -0.3 puts the earlier value at 6.6% in February, which fits.

May 2014 is the best match.

